  {
   "name": "longer",
   "source": "prose written by Claude Sonnet 5.5 for this tool, 2026-10-20",
   "text": "The railway workshop stands at the end of the line, where the tracks fan out into sidings and the grass grows between the sleepers. Before dawn the lamps are lit in the long iron shed, and the first shift of fitters walks in out of the cold with their tin boxes under their arms. They hang their coats on the hooks beside the tool racks, and each man checks that his own spanners are where he left them. A locomotive waits on the pit road with its fire dropped and its boiler still warm, and a boy climbs along its frames with a can of oil, feeding every joint in turn.\n\nThe foreman keeps a board by the door with chalk marks for every engine in the yard. A circle means that the engine is waiting for a part. A cross means that it is ready for the road. A line means that the men are working on it now. He rubs out the marks with his sleeve and writes them again as the day goes on, and the men glance at the board when they pass, as sailors glance at the sky. Nobody remembers who first drew the system, and nobody has ever proposed to change it.\n\nIn the machine bay the lathes turn all day, and the floor shakes a little under the heavy cuts. A turner named Okafor is making a new axle box from a block of bronze. He measures it with calipers every few minutes and speaks to nobody. Beside him an apprentice sharpens a cutting tool on a wheel, throwing a fan of orange sparks across the concrete. When the axle box is done, it will be carried across the yard on a handcart, fitted to the wheel, and tested with a hammer. A good fit rings like a bell. A bad fit gives a dull note, and the work begins again.\n\nAt noon the whistle blows, and the men sit on upturned crates in the sun to eat bread and cold meat. They talk about the price of coal, the football on Saturday, and the new timetable that the company has promised for the spring. The older men do not trust the timetable. They have seen three of them come and go, and each one was drawn by a clerk who had never stood on a platform in the rain. The younger men laugh at this, but they listen, because the older men are right more often than not.\n\nIn the afternoon a train comes in late from the north, and the whole yard stirs. The driver leans from the cab and shouts that the brakes are binding on the third coach. Two fitters run along the track with their bags, and a third unhooks the coach and rolls it onto the siding by hand. They find a cracked shoe and a worn pin, and within the hour they have replaced both. The driver signs the slip without reading it, because he knows these men, and he knows that the coach will go out tonight as sound as the day it was built.",
